//! The maps.
//!
//! Defined as data in the shared crate rather than as client-side art, because
//! the server collides against it and traces shots through it. If the client
//! held a different map from the server, players would be shot through walls
//! that only one side believes in.
//!
//! Two are built: `arena`, the thirty-metre deathmatch box, and `yard`, a
//! much larger open map. The server runs every map at once - each match holds
//! its own `&'static Map` - and `MatchStarted` names the ground, so the client
//! draws the model the server is colliding against rather than whichever one
//! it happened to cache.
//!
//! # Where these numbers come from
//!
//! The brushes below are generated, by `scripts/derive-brushes.py`, from the
//! same `assets/maps/<name>.glb` the client draws. That is the whole point: the
//! client no longer draws the brushes, it draws a model, so the two can drift
//! apart in a way they could not when the arena was built by hand. Deriving one
//! from the other is what keeps them honest. Edit the model and regenerate;
//! do not edit the table.
//!
//! Everything is still axis-aligned boxes. That is a real constraint on the
//! shapes available - no ramps, no angles - but it keeps collision simple
//! enough to be obviously correct, which matters more here than architectural
//! variety. The generator turns slopes into steps and rounded props into boxes.
//!
//! # Two kinds of brush
//!
//! Most of this table is props - crates, barrels, pillars - and each one is the
//! exact bounding box of the mesh that draws it. Anything hollow enough to
//! stand inside is not a prop, however box-shaped it looks from outside. Nothing is
//! rounded to a grid and nothing is left out, so what a player runs into is
//! the size it looks.
//!
//! The rest is structure: the walls, the building in the middle, the staircases
//! and ramps, none of which fill their own bounding box. Those are voxelised
//! into a height field, which is why you can walk into the building but not
//! stand on its roof, and why you cannot walk under something you can climb
//! onto.
//!
//! Brushes are allowed to overlap. They used to be forbidden from it, because
//! the client drew them; it draws the model now, so two boxes sharing a volume
//! costs nothing and the union is exactly the right shape.
//!
//! `no_brush_is_absurd` is what guards the classification: a mesh wrongly taken
//! for a prop would become a single box swallowing the arena, and that is the
//! one mistake this file could make that a player would not survive.

use glam::Vec3;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};

/// An axis-aligned solid block.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Brush {
    pub min: Vec3,
    pub max: Vec3,
}

impl Brush {
    pub const fn new(min: Vec3, max: Vec3) -> Self {
        Self { min, max }
    }

    pub fn size(&self) -> Vec3 {
        self.max - self.min
    }

    pub fn centre(&self) -> Vec3 {
        (self.min + self.max) * 0.5
    }
}

/// Where a player is placed when they spawn.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct Spawn {
    pub position: Vec3,
    /// Facing, in radians.
    pub yaw: f32,
}

pub struct Map {
    /// What the server calls this map in the handshake, and what the client
    /// uses to pick which model to draw.
    pub name: &'static str,
    /// What the client must scale `assets/maps/<name>.glb` by when drawing it.
    ///
    /// Per map rather than global: the arena is authored small and played at
    /// four times size, while the yard is already roughly in metres. Drawing
    /// either at the wrong scale would put every wall somewhere the server does
    /// not think it is.
    pub scale: f32,
    /// Half the playable extent, for bounds checks.
    pub half_x: f32,
    pub half_z: f32,
    pub brushes: &'static [Brush],
    pub spawns: &'static [Spawn],
    /// How many players one match on this map seats.
    ///
    /// A property of the map rather than of the game: the yard is 252 metres
    /// across and swallows thirty players, while thirty in the arena would be
    /// a scrum. There are always more spawns than this, so a full match still
    /// has spare places to scatter people across.
    pub max_players: usize,
    /// Built the first time anything asks the map a spatial question, and
    /// shared from then on. `OnceLock` rather than a table generated alongside
    /// the brushes because the index is derived data with no business being in
    /// source control, and rebuilding it costs microseconds once.
    index: std::sync::OnceLock<crate::sim::broadphase::Broadphase>,
}

impl Map {
    #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]
    pub const fn new(
        name: &'static str,
        scale: f32,
        half_x: f32,
        half_z: f32,
        brushes: &'static [Brush],
        spawns: &'static [Spawn],
        max_players: usize,
    ) -> Self {
        Self {
            name,
            scale,
            half_x,
            half_z,
            brushes,
            spawns,
            max_players,
            index: std::sync::OnceLock::new(),
        }
    }

    /// The radius that covers this whole map, corners included.
    ///
    /// Where the match's circle starts. Taken from the map's own half-extents
    /// rather than written down per map, so a map that grows - and the arena
    /// has - does not quietly start its first minute with a wall through the
    /// middle of its new half.
    pub fn full_radius(&self) -> f32 {
        (self.half_x * self.half_x + self.half_z * self.half_z).sqrt()
    }

    /// A map for a test that only cares about its geometry.
    ///
    /// The name, the scale and the extents are what the client and the
    /// containment tests read; a fixture asserting something about sliding
    /// along a wall or tracing a shot reads none of them. Spelling them out at
    /// every fixture would be four numbers of noise apiece, every one of them
    /// untrue, and a reader would have to check each before ignoring it.
    #[cfg(test)]
    pub const fn fixture(brushes: &'static [Brush], spawns: &'static [Spawn]) -> Self {
        // Extents far larger than any fixture, so nothing is accidentally
        // out of bounds.
        Self::new("fixture", 1.0, 1.0e4, 1.0e4, brushes, spawns, 2)
    }

    /// The brushes that might overlap a box, rather than all of them.
    ///
    /// Every caller still tests what it is handed - this only narrows the
    /// field. See `broadphase` for why that distinction is the whole safety
    /// argument.
    pub fn near<F>(&self, min: Vec3, max: Vec3, visit: F)
    where
        F: FnMut(usize, &Brush) -> bool,
    {
        self.index
            .get_or_init(|| crate::sim::broadphase::Broadphase::build(self.brushes))
            .for_each_near(self.brushes, min, max, visit);
    }
    /// One spawn by index, wrapping.
    ///
    /// Callers that are placing a whole match should use [`Map::scatter`]
    /// instead: this wraps, so asking for more than there are hands the same
    /// point out twice and puts two players inside each other.
    pub fn spawn(&self, index: usize) -> Spawn {
        self.spawns[index % self.spawns.len()]
    }

    /// `count` distinct spawns, in a different arrangement every match.
    ///
    /// Distinct because two players in one spot is two players who cannot
    /// miss, and shuffled because a fixed order means the same people meet
    /// the same corner of the map every time. There are more spawns than a
    /// match seats precisely so that this has something to choose between.
    ///
    /// The shuffle is a Fisher-Yates over the indices, taking randomness from
    /// the caller. The simulation has no source of its own and must not grow
    /// one: it runs on the server and, compiled to wasm, in every client, and
    /// a function that reached for the clock or the OS would be a function
    /// whose result differed between the two.
    pub fn scatter(&self, count: usize, mut random: impl FnMut() -> u32) -> Vec<Spawn> {
        let mut order: Vec<usize> = (0..self.spawns.len()).collect();
        for i in (1..order.len()).rev() {
            let j = (random() as usize) % (i + 1);
            order.swap(i, j);
        }
        order
            .into_iter()
            .take(count.min(self.spawns.len()))
            .map(|i| self.spawns[i])
            .collect()
    }
}

/// Shorthands for the generated tables below: the two corners of a box, and a
/// spawn's position and facing.
///
/// These exist so that one brush fits on one line. Spelled out, rustfmt splits
/// every entry across four lines and a hundred and forty brushes becomes six
/// hundred lines of noise that no one can scan and every regeneration rewrites.
const fn brush(x0: f32, y0: f32, z0: f32, x1: f32, y1: f32, z1: f32) -> Brush {
    Brush::new(Vec3::new(x0, y0, z0), Vec3::new(x1, y1, z1))
}

/// One eighth of a turn. Spawn facings are whole eighths, so they are written
/// as multiples of this rather than as decimals - which is both what they are
/// and what stops clippy observing that 0.7854 is approximately pi/4.
const EIGHTH: f32 = std::f32::consts::FRAC_PI_4;

const fn spawn(x: f32, y: f32, z: f32, yaw: f32) -> Spawn {
    Spawn {
        position: Vec3::new(x, y, z),
        yaw,
    }
}

/// How many brushes at the head of the table are the stated perimeter rather
/// than derived geometry. The tests skip them: they are deliberately larger
/// than anything in the art, because they are what stops a player leaving.
#[cfg(test)]
const PERIMETER_BRUSHES: usize = 5;

/// Bumped whenever the geometry changes. The server sends it in the handshake,
/// so a client running an older map is told to reload rather than silently
/// colliding against different walls.
///
/// Version 4 was the first arena built from art rather than from brushes.
/// Version 5 made its stairs and ledges climbable, 6 made everything solid,
/// 7 gave every prop its own exact box and scaled the arena up, 8 stopped
/// treating open containers as solid blocks you could see into but not enter,
/// 9 added a second map and redecomposed both with greedy meshing, 10 put
/// every surface at the height it is drawn at rather than snapping it to one
/// of three and gave walls above the ground floor collision at all, and 11
/// stopped spawning players in pockets they cannot walk out of and stopped a
/// prop's collision being one box from the ground to its roof, and 12 stopped
/// filling every roofed room solid from its floor to its own ceiling, and 13
/// closed the gaps under stair treads that change opened, stopped slicing a
/// prop into steps taller than a player can climb, and pointed every spawn
/// into the map rather than at whatever it happened to face, and 14 drew the
/// arena half again as large so its buildings stand over a player rather
/// than beside them, with the step height raised to match so that climbing
/// came out where it was, and 15 stopped recording every surface a quarter
/// of a metre above where it is drawn, which is what made the yard's white
/// staircase unclimbable from the bottom, and 16 spread the spawns over the
/// whole of each map again by asking about connectivity at the player's own
/// width rather than at the spawn margin, and 17 made every spawn face the
/// middle of the map rather than whichever direction had the most empty
/// ground in front of it, and 18 gave the arena a second half: the east
/// wall is now a gated boundary rather than the edge of the world, with
/// thirty metres of new ground, six buildings and a way onto every one of
/// their roofs behind it. The same change stopped `harmonise` moving a cell
/// more than three bands - unbounded, it had been quietly turning
/// staircases into nine metre towers whenever `climb` misjudged one - and
/// made `climb` settle each column at its lowest reachable surface instead
/// of whichever the flood happened to reach first, so that adding geometry
/// can no longer take reachability away somewhere else, and 19 sealed the
/// bare slab outside the art. The perimeter is a box around the bounding
/// box and the art inside it is not a box; the 3,279 m2 of stated floor
/// left between them on the yard was standable, reachable by walking off a
/// ten metre roof over a boundary wall that drops to 6.75 m, and had open
/// water past its edge. 19 shipped that with a bad flood behind it and 20
/// is the repair: a ground plane is two enormous triangles and the
/// voxeliser samples a triangle at most 600 steps a side, so open ground
/// comes out as a lattice of unmarked cells, and treating those as
/// "outside the map" put a comb of invisible fourteen-metre walls across
/// the middle of the yard. The flood is opened against that now. 21 put a
/// guardrail on the outer apron - the drawn but empty ground between the
/// last building and the water, which a player could be knocked onto and
/// then spend the round behind the map - and dropped the arena's redundant
/// red-orange floor quad, which had been z-fighting with its grey ground
/// plane over the whole map. 23 hid `room_0`'s own floor, which did the
/// same inside that building; the collision tables came out unchanged, so
/// this bump is for the model alone. 29 sank the facility's water tower
/// until all four of its legs reach the ground; three had hung in the air.
pub const MAP_VERSION: u32 = 29;

